{"attack": true, "case": "The target conflates 'verisimilitude' (proximity to truth) with 'informational content' / 'error-detection capacity,' treating them as co-varying or interchangeable, when they are logically independent properties. The claim frames the defense as maximizing both 'verisimilitude (truth-likeness) and informational content' as a package, and the mechanism equates selecting 'the theory with the highest potential truth-content (verisimilitude)' with choosing 'the most informative error-detector available.' But a theory can have maximal informational content (ruling out the most logical space, making it the sharpest error-detector) while being maximally distant from the truth (minimal verisimilitude), and vice versa. Popper himself distinguished content (which determines testability) from verisimilitude (which is a function of truth-content minus falsity-content). By silently substituting the pragmatic property (informational content / error-detection utility) for the epistemic property (verisimilitude), the target's 'non-inductive' defense only establishes that corroboration tracks the former — it never bridges to the latter. Claiming that preferring corroborated theories maximizes verisimilitude would require an inductive step from past test performance to current truth-likeness, which is exactly what the defense purports to avoid. Thus the defense is a bait-and-switch: it defends the preference on informational-content grounds but labels it a 'verisimilitude' defense. Additionally, the mechanism's final sentence ('Unlike every previously studied system of this kind, which faced identical structural constraints yet exhibited none of this behaviour') is contextually nonsensical boilerplate — the target is a philosophical argument about Popperian epistemology, not a 'system' with 'previously studied systems of this kind,' suggesting the artifact contains templated content irrelevant to its substance.", "counterexample": [{"claim": "The theory 'All